@hawtcode I'm not sure why you think the true fact that "prediction can recover and generalize over only the latent structure that's identifiable in the information channels" excludes eg weather data. The latent structure in weather data is the actual laws of physics that produce the weather (proof of concept - if every time the pressure is low, it rains the next day, an AI pretrained on weather data could recover this structure). This is, if anything, easier to model than human intelligence (meteorology is a more advanced science than psychology or AI!) This is imho all that humans are doing. We see weather happening, we look over past weather data, and we form theories about what hidden structure produced that data (ie what weather is). An AI can do that from text about weather just as well as we can do it by feeling the raindrops on our skin (except insofar as there's something about weather we failed to record in our text).

You say that "humans are embodied, have goal-oriented behavior, apply judgment, update their models instantaneously, maintain persistent context, [etc]". About a third of these are already true of AIs too - AIs have goal-oriented behavior, just read about the Hugging Face hack - those guys obviously wanted to do really well on their benchmark! Another third are things people are working on that I expect to be done within the next few years - I think "persistent context" is pretty similar to what's measured by the "time horizon" stat that doubles every few months. The rest of these I think are red herrings. Yeah, people thought "embodiment" was a big deal ten years ago, but I think it seems more like "AI proves embodiment was never as important as we thought" than "lack of embodiment proves AI can't be intelligent". There are lots of boring random contingent properties of humans that AIs don't have - AIs don't have hair, AIs don't breathe oxygen - but we shouldn't assume these are necessary to intelligence unless we have a theory of intelligence which provides some evidence that they matter, and AFAICT the predictive coding theory is the best one that psychology can come up with right now and it says they don't.

I think "bounded" vs. "unbounded" domain is too black-and-white. Tic-tac-toe is more bounded than chess is more bounded than Go is more bounded than Minecraft is more bounded than scientific research is more bounded than Reality. We've seen AI expand into gradually less bounded domains, and as far as I can tell this is just the scaling laws - Astra was able to play a bunch of free-form video games it had never seen before, because the bounds relaxed to the point where the video games were no longer too unbounded for it to handle. I don't know how many orders of magnitude more complicated scientific research is compared to Minecraft, but I think it's some finite amount, and we'll get AIs that can do 10% of it before AIs that can do 20% of it before AIs that can do 100% of it.
